\title{Two small extremal graphs from the triangle-free degree bound}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We recover two exact entries in the tables of Goedgebeur and Jooken by a
classical degree argument. A connected graph of degree six, girth four,
and respectively twenty or sixteen shortest cycles through each edge has
minimum order fourteen or fifteen. In each case the graph of minimum order
is unique. The proof uses the triangle-free case of the
Andr\'asfai--Erd\H{o}s--S\'os theorem, including its equality case; no search
through regular graphs is required.
\end{abstract}

\section{Scope and attribution}
All graphs in this note are finite, simple and undirected. An
\emph{edge-girth-regular} graph with parameters $(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is a connected
$k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices, of girth $g$, with exactly $\lambda$ cycles
of length $g$ through each edge. We write $n(k,g,\lambda)$ for the least such
order, when it exists. Isomorphisms do not preserve a distinguished edge or
vertex.

The two classifications proved below already appear in Table~4 of the fixed
edition
\nolinkurl{arXiv:2401.08271v2} (25 June 2024) of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}.
Their table credits its earlier lower bounds to their reference~[8], and the
fourteen-vertex upper bound to their reference~[15, Example~5.1]. Those
attributions remain with the original table. The corresponding stored graphs
are \nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_38} and \nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_43} in the authors'
subsequent repository snapshot of December 2024. We distinguish that snapshot
from the fixed manuscript edition.

The degree bound and its extremal graph are classical~\cite{AES}. In fact the
shortest odd cycle argument in Lemma~\ref{lem:degree} is the argument of their
Lemma~1.2, specialized to regular graphs; the equality description is the
$r=3$ case of their Theorem~1.1. We include the details needed here. The
contribution of this note is the direct application, with an explicit
identification of both table entries and independently checkable maps to the
stored examples. We claim neither a new extremal theorem nor a new answer
to an open problem.

\section{The degree bound and its equality case}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:degree}
Let $X$ be a triangle-free $k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices, where $k>0$.
If $X$ is not bipartite, then $n\geq 5k/2$. If equality holds, then $k$ is
even and $X$ is obtained from a pentagon by replacing each vertex by an
independent set of $k/2$ vertices, with every possible edge between
consecutive sets.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Take an odd cycle $C$ of shortest length $\ell$. Triangle-freeness gives
$\ell\geq5$. A chord would split $C$ into two cycles, one odd and shorter
than $C$, so $C$ has no chord.

A vertex outside $C$ has at most two neighbours on $C$. Indeed, suppose
it has $t\geq3$ such neighbours. Their cyclic successive distances on $C$
are at least two, since a distance of one would give a triangle. They sum
to the odd integer $\ell$, so one distance $d$ is odd. The other at least
two distances sum to at least four, whence $d\leq\ell-4$. The path of
length $d$ on $C$, together with the two edges to the outside vertex,
would be an odd cycle of length $d+2<\ell$, a contradiction. A vertex
on $C$ has exactly two neighbours on $C$ because there are no chords.
Counting incidences with $C$ now gives
\[
 \ell k=\sum_{v\in V(X)}|N(v)\cap V(C)|\leq 2n.
\]
Thus $n\geq\ell k/2\geq5k/2$.

Suppose $n=5k/2$. Both inequalities are equalities, so $\ell=5$ and every
vertex has exactly two neighbours on $C$. Write $C=(c_0,\ldots,c_4)$,
with suffixes modulo five. The two neighbours cannot be consecutive,
and hence have the form $\{c_{i-1},c_{i+1}\}$ for a unique $i$. Put
\[
 V_i=\{v:N(v)\cap V(C)=\{c_{i-1},c_{i+1}\}\},\qquad a_i=|V_i|.
\]
These sets partition $V(X)$, and $c_i\in V_i$, so none is empty.
Two vertices in $V_i$, or in $V_i$ and $V_{i+2}$, have a common neighbour
on $C$ distinct from both of them. They cannot be adjacent. Consequently
all neighbours of a vertex in $V_i$ lie in $V_{i-1}\cup V_{i+1}$, and
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}\geq k\quad (0\leq i<5).
\]
The sum of these five left sides is $2n=5k$, so every inequality is an
equality. Every vertex is therefore adjacent to every vertex of the two
consecutive parts. Subtracting the equations at $i$ and $i+2$ gives
$a_{i-1}=a_{i+3}$, or $a_j=a_{j-1}$ modulo five. All parts have equal
size, necessarily $k/2$. This proves the assertion.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bipartite}
In a bipartite $k$-regular graph with $k>0$, the two parts have equal size.
A triangle-free $k$-regular graph has at least $2k$ vertices.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Counting edges from either part gives $k|A|=k|B|$. For the second assertion,
choose an edge $uv$. The two neighbourhoods $N(u)$ and $N(v)$ are disjoint,
since a common neighbour would give a triangle. Their union has size $2k$.
\end{proof}

\section{The two classifications}
For $m\geq3$, let $Q_m$ be the graph obtained from $K_{m,m}$ by deleting
a perfect matching. Label its parts $u_1,\ldots,u_m$ and
$v_1,\ldots,v_m$, with $u_i$ adjacent to $v_j$ exactly when $i\ne j$.
It is connected and $(m-1)$-regular. For an edge $u_i v_j$, a four-cycle
is determined by $u_p,v_q$ with
\[
 p\notin\{i,j\},\qquad q\notin\{i,j,p\}.
\]
There are $(m-2)(m-3)$ choices. In particular $Q_7$ has parameters
$(14,6,4,20)$.

For $a\geq2$, let $P_a$ be the graph whose five independent parts
$V_0,\ldots,V_4$ all have size $a$, with all edges between consecutive
parts. It is connected, $2a$-regular and triangle-free. Fix $u\in V_0$
and $v\in V_1$. A four-cycle through $uv$ is a path $u,x,y,v$ together
with $vu$. Its internal parts are respectively
\[
 (V_1,V_0),\quad(V_4,V_0),\quad(V_1,V_2).
\]
There are $(a-1)^2$, $a(a-1)$ and $a(a-1)$ choices, respectively.
The fourth possible pair $(V_4,V_2)$ has no edge. Thus the number is
$(a-1)(3a-1)$, and $P_3$ has parameters $(15,6,4,16)$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:entries}
The following minima and numbers of isomorphism classes at the minimum hold:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
(k,g,\lambda)&n(k,g,\lambda)&\text{number}&\text{representative}\\\hline
(6,4,20)&14&1&Q_7\\
(6,4,16)&15&1&P_3.
\end{array}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $X$ have degree six and girth four. Lemma~\ref{lem:bipartite} gives
$n\geq12$. When $n<15$, Lemma~\ref{lem:degree} makes $X$ bipartite, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:bipartite} makes $n$ even. At $n=12$, both parts have
six vertices; regularity forces $X=K_{6,6}$, with $25$ four-cycles through
each edge. At $n=14$, both parts have seven vertices and every vertex
misses exactly one vertex of the opposite part. The missing pairs form
a perfect matching, so $X\cong Q_7$, which has $20$ four-cycles through
each edge. This proves the first row and excludes all orders below fifteen
for the second row.

At $n=15$ a regular bipartite graph is impossible. The equality case of
Lemma~\ref{lem:degree} therefore forces $X\cong P_3$. Its verified cycle
count is sixteen, proving existence and uniqueness for the second row.
\end{proof}

\section{Certificates and limits}
The accompanying verifier constructs $Q_7$ and $P_3$ directly. It checks
simplicity, connectedness, degrees and the absence of triangles. For every
edge it counts four-cycles in two ways: by length-three paths between its
endpoints, and by a separate enumeration of four-vertex subsets. It also
verifies every pair under the recorded bijections to the two stored graph6
examples. The map provider is not a mathematical premise; the literal
bijections are checked without a graph-isomorphism library. Family checks
for $Q_m$, $4\leq m\leq9$, and $P_a$, $2\leq a\leq5$, exercise the
closed formulae. These finite checks support the examples, while the proof
above supplies completeness. They do not constitute a census of regular
graphs or a Lean formalization.

